completely consistent running

00:08:00.927 --> 00:08:02.700
across both cloud and prem.

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:06.540
So this is the overview
of Anthos, right--

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:09.350
a managed platform
from Google that

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:12.730
allows you to do
application modernization.

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:15.550
This application-- it allows you
to do application modernization

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:16.340
in place.

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:19.630
So in the past, when you were
looking to do modernization,

00:08:19.630 --> 00:08:24.110
typically you do the development
on Google Cloud or GKE.

00:08:24.110 --> 00:08:26.680
But now if you don't even want
your developers-- if customers

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:28.680
don't want developers--
to develop on the cloud,

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:31.973
you can actually do entire
development in the premise.

00:08:31.973 --> 00:08:33.640
But if you want to
develop in the cloud,

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:36.640
you can develop in the cloud
and deploy into the premise

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:38.570
or the other way around.

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:40.780
It's completely
seamless and secure,

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:42.832
unlocking developers
from worrying

00:08:42.832 --> 00:08:44.290
about which cloud
you're running on

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:46.040
or which infrastructure
you're running on.

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:47.920
The choice of where
to run depends

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:50.350
on the policy set
by your enterprise--

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:51.930
by your customer,
your IT policy,

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:54.520
StackOps policy will
decide where you're run it.

00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:56.240
But it unshackles
the developers,

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:58.060
because now you don't have to
worry about where it's running.

00:08:58.060 --> 00:08:59.860
So it's write once,
deploy anywhere.

00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:02.020
And all the security
capabilities

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:03.902
required by IT and
StackOps in order

00:09:03.902 --> 00:09:05.921
to run in an
enterprise environment.

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:11.830
So just to summarize
the value proposition

00:09:11.830 --> 00:09:17.050
both for IT ops, DevOps,
and for developers.

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:19.930
Really it's a
managed environment,

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:24.130
run on-prem or run in the cloud,
fully secure, fully portable,

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:25.820
supported by Google.

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:30.780
And this is the key value
proposition of Anthos.

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:35.670
Now what has Cisco being doing
together with Google Cloud?

00:09:35.670 --> 00:09:40.650
So as customers are looking
to adopt a hybrid cloud,

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:43.120
there's a number of
challenges that block or deter

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:44.530
hybrid cloud adoption.

00:09:44.530 --> 00:09:46.780
The first thing is that they
have existing data center

00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:48.670
infrastructure, which
may be legacy data center

00:09:48.670 --> 00:09:49.340
infrastructure.

00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:52.250
There's a need to modernize
data center infrastructure.

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:53.710
The second thing
is the data center

00:09:53.710 --> 00:09:57.210
is not a place in
the network anymore.

00:09:57.210 --> 00:09:59.440
It's not a fixed location,
because increasingly,

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:01.570
with the adoption
of edge computing,

00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:04.733
your T-type use cases
branch off as use cases.

00:10:04.733 --> 00:10:06.400
The data center is
actually distributed.

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:08.707
Cisco likes to refer to it
as a data center anywhere,

00:10:08.707 --> 00:10:09.790
or data center everywhere.

00:10:09.790 --> 00:10:11.920
Because now you
take your compute

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:13.840
to where the data
is, because you

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:16.270
need to be able to process
data, without having

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:19.840
to transport large volumes
of data across low WAN links.

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:22.600
So there are use cases where
you need to do edge computing.

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:24.400
And so how do you
solve the challenge

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:26.830
when you need to do
edge computing at scale

00:10:26.830 --> 00:10:29.090
and be able to run these
modernized applications

00:10:29.090 --> 00:10:29.590
on them.

00:10:29.590 --> 00:10:32.170
So there are some challenges
in evolving data center

00:10:32.170 --> 00:10:32.772
requirements.

00:10:32.772 --> 00:10:34.480
And really what
customers are looking for

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:36.580
is a private cloud or a
distributed private cloud.

00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:39.070
Because now the private cloud
could be in one data center,

00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:41.710
across multiple data centers,
or across edge locations.

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:44.440
And you need to have a
distributed private cloud,

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:46.480
which looks and feels
just like the public cloud

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:49.328
environment when you're
deploying applications.

00:10:49.328 --> 00:10:50.870
We've talked about
security, and I'll

00:10:50.870 --> 00:10:53.430
talk about one particular
use case with security.

00:10:53.430 --> 00:10:56.270
Especially when it comes to
edge and branch locations,

00:10:56.270 --> 00:10:58.430
having the ability to
have secure connectivity

00:10:58.430 --> 00:11:00.650
between the edge to the
data center to the cloud--

00:11:00.650 --> 00:11:03.020
and having ubiquitous
security across net.

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:05.420
And threat visibility across
this hybrid environment

00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:07.410
is a challenge to solve as well.

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:09.750
And all of this
needs to be managed.

00:11:09.750 --> 00:11:11.990
So what we've done,
together with Google Cloud,

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:15.980
is develop a reference
architecture for Anthos.

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:17.540
What that reference
architecture does

00:11:17.540 --> 00:11:19.790
is it solves all of
these challenges that

00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:22.470
deter hybrid cloud adoption.

00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:25.580
So starting with a
private cloud stack,

00:11:25.580 --> 00:11:29.220
based on Cisco
HyperFlex and Cisco ACI.

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:30.830
So Cisco HyperFlex
and ACI-- this

00:11:30.830 --> 00:11:32.780
is part of the
market-leading product data

00:11:32.780 --> 00:11:35.870
center, product portfolio,
that we've developed.

00:11:35.870 --> 00:11:37.460
And for those of
you who don't know,

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:39.830
Cisco HyperFlex is a
hyper-converged infrastructure

00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:42.140
platform-- a market-leading
hyper-converged platform,

00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:45.120
available for several years now.

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:46.940
But what HyperFlex
allows you to do

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:51.680
is effectively build scalable
private clouds on premise.

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:54.660
It can scale up to very
large call data centers.

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:58.320
It can scale down to edge
locations or robo-locations--

00:11:58.320 --> 00:11:59.990
IoT use cases.

00:11:59.990 --> 00:12:03.410
So it's really a scale out,
hyper-converged architecture

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:05.990
that allows you to build
very efficient, very reliable

00:12:05.990 --> 00:12:08.420
private clouds in the premise.

00:12:08.420 --> 00:12:12.230
And ACI is Cisco's data
center SDN solution,

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:14.720
which gives you the ability
of doing network automation--

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:17.660
network provisioning--
at scale and having

00:12:17.660 --> 00:12:20.030
the ability to automate
across multiple layers

00:12:20.030 --> 00:12:21.580
of the networking stack.

00:12:21.580 --> 00:12:23.270
underlay networking,
overlay networking,

00:12:23.270 --> 00:12:25.570
container networking,
service mesh networking--

00:12:25.570 --> 00:12:27.770
all these layers of
the networking stack.

00:12:27.770 --> 00:12:29.878
You need a simple way
to be able to manage

00:12:29.878 --> 00:12:31.170
different layers of networking.

00:12:31.170 --> 00:12:32.970
And that's where ACI comes in.

00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:35.390
So now this combination
of HyperFlex and ACI

00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:39.230
gives you a very reliable,
scalable, simple, private cloud

00:12:39.230 --> 00:12:43.760
for customers wanting
to run GKE on-premise.

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:47.150
The second is Cisco's
SD-WAN technology,

00:12:47.150 --> 00:12:50.480
which allows customers
to securely extend

00:12:50.480 --> 00:12:53.000
their environments, whether
it's a data center or branch

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:55.820
and be able to securely extend
that environment across VPNs

00:12:55.820 --> 00:12:58.335
and be able to connect
into, for example, the VPC.

00:12:58.335 --> 00:13:00.500
If you've got applications
running in the VPC,

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:02.450
being able to create
secure endpoints

00:13:02.450 --> 00:13:08.600
in the cloud, which are VPN
extensions from the premise.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:12.260
Cisco's Stealthwatch cloud
is, again, a leading solution

00:13:12.260 --> 00:13:14.840
to be able to do threat
detection-- threat monitoring--

00:13:14.840 --> 00:13:16.100
in a hybrid environment.

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:19.270
It natively integrates with
Google Cloud VPC Flow Logs,

00:13:19.270 --> 00:13:22.610
so you can monitor traffic
patterns-- traffic attacks--

00:13:22.610 --> 00:13:24.620
coming in the cloud
or in the prem

00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:27.650
and actually give customers
the ability to monitor threats

00:13:27.650 --> 00:13:30.630
across this hybrid environment.

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:33.810
And then Intersight a SaaS-based
infrastructure management

00:13:33.810 --> 00:13:35.910
platform from Cisco
that allows customers

00:13:35.910 --> 00:13:38.110
to manage this private cloud.

00:13:38.110 --> 00:13:41.940
So together this combination--
this reference architecture--

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:46.110
of Cisco Data Center technology
with the Cisco networking

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:50.310
and security portfolio
combined with Anthos

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:54.030
gives a very simple
platform for customers

00:13:54.030 --> 00:13:56.400
looking to adopt hybrid
cloud, making it easier

00:13:56.400 --> 00:14:00.180
for them to build,
connect, protect, and scale

00:14:00.180 --> 00:14:03.040
their hybrid cloud deployments.

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:05.610
So this is the overall
reference architecture.

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:06.870
Those of you who are taking
pictures, it's probably

00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:09.120
a good time to take the
picture, because now the build

00:14:09.120 --> 00:14:09.670
is complete.

00:14:09.670 --> 00:14:11.760
So feel free to go ahead
and take pictures now.

00:14:18.450 --> 00:14:20.800
All right, let's keep going.

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:23.230
So what does this mean
to you as customers?

00:14:23.230 --> 00:14:23.950
As developers?

00:14:23.950 --> 00:14:27.340
As customers, what
does this give you?

00:14:27.340 --> 00:14:28.420
What are the benefits?

00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:30.628
And what is the differentiation
that Cisco and Google

00:14:30.628 --> 00:14:33.590
Cloud are developing together.

00:14:33.590 --> 00:14:36.562
Number one-- we are focusing
on turnkey operations.

00:14:36.562 --> 00:14:39.020
We want the whole experience
to bring up the infrastructure

00:14:39.020 --> 00:14:40.760
to be really simple.

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:43.790
So what we are doing, working
together with Google Cloud,

00:14:43.790 --> 00:14:46.980
is integrating Anthos into
Cisco's management platforms.

00:14:46.980 --> 00:14:50.150
So when a customer wants to
bring up an instance of GKE

00:14:50.150 --> 00:14:53.840
on prem, it's very simplified,
turnkey, validated,

00:14:53.840 --> 00:14:55.740
out-of-the-box,
fully-integrated,

00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:57.200
and it comes up.

00:14:57.200 --> 00:14:59.150
And you can stand
up a cloud on prem

00:14:59.150 --> 00:15:02.640
in a very, very short time.

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:06.450
Now, HyperFlex is a
data center solution

00:15:06.450 --> 00:15:09.300
that integrates both
compute and storage

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:11.700
and software-defined storage,
software-defined computer

00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:13.210
scale-out solution.

00:15:13.210 --> 00:15:16.170
But it gives you an
enterprise-grade private cloud

00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:19.260
platform, capable of providing
enterprise-grade compute

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:21.690
and storage services
to Kubernetes.

00:15:21.690 --> 00:15:23.552
Even more specifically,
Kubernetes--

00:15:23.552 --> 00:15:26.010
those of you who are familiar
with the storage architecture

00:15:26.010 --> 00:15:28.860
of Kubernetes-- there is a newly
developed container storage

00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:31.710
interface, or the CSI
interface for Kubernetes.

00:15:31.710 --> 00:15:33.660
And what we're able
to do with HyperFlex

00:15:33.660 --> 00:15:36.000
is provide that storage--
that CSI interface--

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:38.070
for persistent
volumes in Kubernetes,

00:15:38.070 --> 00:15:40.440
running on our hyperconverged
infrastructure.

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:42.100
And that's what we're doing.

00:15:42.100 --> 00:15:44.970
So what that allows you
to do in the platform is

00:15:44.970 --> 00:15:46.860
enterprise-grade,
highly-resilient,

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:49.320
highly-clustered, and so now
when applications are running

00:15:49.320 --> 00:15:51.210
on that, they're running
with full-resiliency

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:54.840
in an enterprise-grade,
hyperconverged platform.

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:57.660
And I mentioned, now,
networking a couple of times.

00:15:57.660 --> 00:16:00.750
But again, this integrated
networking and security

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:04.620
into the platform, really gives
us an end-to-end architecture

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:07.320
for security.

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:09.900
And the entire
solution is jointly

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:11.773
integrated, jointly validated.

00:16:11.773 --> 00:16:14.190
You will see reference designs--
reference architectures--

00:16:14.190 --> 00:16:16.740
that are going to be published
by Cisco and Google Cloud.

00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:18.995
And we are doing a lot
of product integration

00:16:18.995 --> 00:16:19.620
in the backend.

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:22.553
And over the next few months,
you will see more and more

00:16:22.553 --> 00:16:24.720
announcements that we're
going to make about further

00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:26.262
integrations--
tighter integrations--

00:16:26.262 --> 00:16:28.590
that we're doing to
create a really simple,

00:16:28.590 --> 00:16:31.548
out-of-the-box cloud for
customers wanting to run

00:16:31.548 --> 00:16:32.340
Kubernetes on prem.

00:16:36.430 --> 00:16:39.102
Now, just a small
blurb on HyperFlex--

00:16:39.102 --> 00:16:41.560
for those of you who are not
familiar with hyperconvergence

00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:42.310
or with HyperFlex.

00:16:42.310 --> 00:16:44.830
So just a brief product
pitch on what this is.

00:16:44.830 --> 00:16:47.240
I won't spend too much
time on product pitches,

00:16:47.240 --> 00:16:48.870
but just as an introduction.

00:16:48.870 --> 00:16:50.710
Right, one of the
biggest challenges

00:16:50.710 --> 00:16:52.360
with traditional
data center designs

00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:54.070
is that it's highly
manual to operate

00:16:54.070 --> 00:16:56.830
and very, very cumbersome
to build and operate

00:16:56.830 --> 00:16:58.820
traditional data center designs.

00:16:58.820 --> 00:17:01.210
And that's the reason
technology like hyperconvergence

00:17:01.210 --> 00:17:02.920
has become extremely popular.

00:17:02.920 --> 00:17:04.810
Because what
hyperconvergence does

00:17:04.810 --> 00:17:07.119
is it basically integrates
compute, storage,

00:17:07.119 --> 00:17:12.020
and networking into a seamless
software-defined platform,

00:17:12.020 --> 00:17:15.069
which is very simple to
build, very simply to operate.

00:17:15.069 --> 00:17:18.277
You don't need different
combinations of storage teams

00:17:18.277 --> 00:17:19.069
or networking team.

00:17:19.069 --> 00:17:20.020
It's all very simple.

00:17:20.020 --> 00:17:22.402
It's all built on a
virtualized platform.

00:17:22.402 --> 00:17:24.069
And it basically gives
you-- think of it

00:17:24.069 --> 00:17:25.450
as a cloud in a box, right.

00:17:25.450 --> 00:17:28.180
It's a cloud in a box
in a small environment.

00:17:28.180 --> 00:17:30.892
And because of the clustered
nature of the architecture,

00:17:30.892 --> 00:17:31.600
it can scale out.

00:17:31.600 --> 00:17:33.730
It can start with something
as small as three nodes

00:17:33.730 --> 00:17:36.530
in a cluster and
scale this out to 8,

00:17:36.530 --> 00:17:39.970
16, 32, 64 nodes-- you can build
really, really large clusters.

00:17:39.970 --> 00:17:42.372
And we've got customers
deploying this in hundreds

00:17:42.372 --> 00:17:44.168
in large call data centers.

00:17:44.168 --> 00:17:46.210
At the same time, we've
also developed technology

00:17:46.210 --> 00:17:48.252
to be able to scale it
down to edge environments.

00:17:48.252 --> 00:17:51.197
So it can run in branch
offices and IoT use cases.

00:17:51.197 --> 00:17:52.780
I've got a very,
very interesting demo

00:17:52.780 --> 00:17:54.220
that I want to
show you at the end

00:17:54.220 --> 00:17:57.610
where we are going to use
HyperFlex running in a factory

00:17:57.610 --> 00:18:00.848
floor, doing real-time
inferencing for machine

00:18:00.848 --> 00:18:02.890
learning use cases-- an
industrial automation use

00:18:02.890 --> 00:18:04.265
case, a very
interesting use case

00:18:04.265 --> 00:18:07.420
that I'd like you to pay
attention to at the end.

00:18:07.420 --> 00:18:09.640
And because of
this ability for us

00:18:09.640 --> 00:18:11.260
to build this
ubiquitous architecture

00:18:11.260 --> 00:18:15.160
that spans across the
data center, to the cloud,

00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:16.560
to the edge.

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:18.640
We've redefined
the notion of what

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:20.170
we call the distributed cloud.

00:18:20.170 --> 00:18:23.680
So now what you can think
of is a distributed cloud

00:18:23.680 --> 00:18:26.050
for your modernized
applications-- for Kubernetes

00:18:26.050 --> 00:18:29.260
applications-- and complete
freedom both for developers

00:18:29.260 --> 00:18:32.440
and for the infrastructure
operators on whether you

00:18:32.440 --> 00:18:34.900
want to run the app in the
cloud, in data centers,

00:18:34.900 --> 00:18:35.590
or the edge.

00:18:35.590 --> 00:18:37.673
But you've got a seamless,
ubiquitous architecture

00:18:37.673 --> 00:18:41.207
that allows customers
to do all of these.

00:18:41.207 --> 00:18:43.040
So just to summarize
the value proposition--

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:44.060
just a very simple one.

00:18:44.060 --> 00:18:45.860
Very simple.

00:18:45.860 --> 00:18:50.420
Very simple to operate,
highly reliable architecture.

00:18:50.420 --> 00:18:52.520
And very importantly,
it does allow

00:18:52.520 --> 00:18:57.170
the coexistence of what
analysts call Mode 1 and Mode 2.

00:18:57.170 --> 00:19:00.140
Right, so Mode 1 refers to
traditional IT operations.

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:01.348
Typically these are running--

00:19:01.348 --> 00:19:02.723
traditional
applications, running

00:19:02.723 --> 00:19:04.010
in a virtualized environment.

00:19:04.010 --> 00:19:05.407
Because customers are not--

00:19:05.407 --> 00:19:07.490
you can't modernize
everything at one shot, right?

00:19:07.490 --> 00:19:09.920
So you have-- typically
the databases are still

00:19:09.920 --> 00:19:11.470
running in virtual machines.

00:19:11.470 --> 00:19:13.370
They could be running
in traditional storage.

00:19:13.370 --> 00:19:16.390
And you've got the ability to
have the web front-end start

00:19:16.390 --> 00:19:17.140
to get modernized.

00:19:17.140 --> 00:19:17.630
They get good.

00:19:17.630 --> 00:19:18.560
They get containerized.

00:19:18.560 --> 00:19:20.810
Then the application services
start to get containerized.

00:19:20.810 --> 00:19:22.330
As everything
becomes distributed,

00:19:22.330 --> 00:19:25.160
you have to have the
ability to coexist

00:19:25.160 --> 00:19:27.200
existing, traditional
applications

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:29.815
with the more modernized,
containerized applications.

00:19:29.815 --> 00:19:31.940
And what you're able to do
with this architecture--

00:19:31.940 --> 00:19:33.350
with HyperFlex--
is you can actually

00:19:33.350 --> 00:19:34.650
run them on the
same architecture,

00:19:34.650 --> 00:19:35.640
the same environment.

00:19:35.640 --> 00:19:37.550
So you don't have to do a
forklift upgrade of your data

00:19:37.550 --> 00:19:39.620
center, and you can have
a seamless transition

00:19:39.620 --> 00:19:42.430
between the traditional world
and the modernized application

00:19:42.430 --> 00:19:43.820
world.

00:19:43.820 --> 00:19:45.120
And like I said before--

00:19:45.120 --> 00:19:45.900
highly scalable.

00:19:45.900 --> 00:19:47.840
So it gives you the economics
of the cloud as well.

00:19:47.840 --> 00:19:49.160
So you can build as
you grow, and you

00:19:49.160 --> 00:19:50.868
pay as you grow for
those who are looking

00:19:50.868 --> 00:19:54.370
to start small and evolve.

00:19:54.370 --> 00:19:57.013
Again a very short pitch on ACI.

00:19:57.013 --> 00:19:57.930
All I'm going to say--

00:19:57.930 --> 00:19:59.580
ACI is Cisco's SDN technology.

00:19:59.580 --> 00:20:03.330
And the main reason ACI is
important in the architecture

00:20:03.330 --> 00:20:06.020
is because you've got multiple
layers of a networking stack,

00:20:06.020 --> 00:20:06.520
right.

00:20:06.520 --> 00:20:07.860
There's the physical
networking--

00:20:07.860 --> 00:20:08.760
or the underlay networking.

00:20:08.760 --> 00:20:10.320
You've got the
overlay networking.

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:11.820
And then in the
world of containers,

00:20:11.820 --> 00:20:13.777
you've got the container
networking layer.

00:20:13.777 --> 00:20:15.360
And then you've got
the service layer,

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:16.930
which is at the layer seven.

00:20:16.930 --> 00:20:20.790
So if you have multiple software
stacks or multiple layers

00:20:20.790 --> 00:20:23.760
of the network, troubleshooting
and monitoring and providing

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:25.273
visibility into
that is complicated.

00:20:25.273 --> 00:20:26.690
And what we're
able to do with ACI

00:20:26.690 --> 00:20:28.380
is give a single
networking stack

00:20:28.380 --> 00:20:30.803
that's able to manage both the
physical, overlay container

00:20:30.803 --> 00:20:32.220
networking and
service networking.

00:20:32.220 --> 00:20:34.143
It can all be integrated
into one stack.

00:20:34.143 --> 00:20:36.060
So when you need to
troubleshoot-- when things

00:20:36.060 --> 00:20:38.220
go wrong, it's one
tool that you go

00:20:38.220 --> 00:20:40.627
to to figure out how you
troubleshoot the environment.

00:20:40.627 --> 00:20:43.210
So that's the reason technology
like ACI is really important--

00:20:43.210 --> 00:20:45.335
because it allows you to
create that ubiquity, kind

00:20:45.335 --> 00:20:47.130
of vertical network
stack, that allows

00:20:47.130 --> 00:20:52.000
you to manage all layers of
the networking environment.

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:54.250
I don't know how many of you
are partners in the room.

00:20:54.250 --> 00:20:57.640
But what we've been working
together with our partner--

00:20:57.640 --> 00:21:01.830
with Google Cloud-- is create
very simple ability for managed

00:21:01.830 --> 00:21:02.890
services--

00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:05.848
MSIs, system integrators,
or technology partners--

00:21:05.848 --> 00:21:08.140
to take this joint solution
that I referenced-- designs

00:21:08.140 --> 00:21:11.320
available, blueprints available,
or that will be available.

00:21:11.320 --> 00:21:15.490
And we'll take these and
allow customers to started.

00:21:15.490 --> 00:21:17.140
So you have simple,
starter kits that

00:21:17.140 --> 00:21:18.940
allow customers to get started.

00:21:18.940 --> 00:21:21.550
If you want to actually go
and develop the data centers,

00:21:21.550 --> 00:21:23.860
build the data centers,
develop applications,

00:21:23.860 --> 00:21:25.880
partners can offer
services there.

00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:29.140
And then we're able to implement
a broad set of services

00:21:29.140 --> 00:21:31.890
for application
modernization as well.

00:21:31.890 --> 00:21:35.320
So again, if there's any
partners-- channel partners,

00:21:35.320 --> 00:21:37.300
system integration
partners-- in the room,

00:21:37.300 --> 00:21:40.420
we're working to enable
for you as well how

00:21:40.420 --> 00:21:44.020
to easily consume and pass
on the benefits to your end

00:21:44.020 --> 00:21:47.188
customers.

00:21:47.188 --> 00:21:48.980
I'm going to switch
gears a little bit now.

00:21:48.980 --> 00:21:51.540
I talked about the technology.

00:21:51.540 --> 00:21:53.840
Now I think this is the
more interesting part of it.

00:21:53.840 --> 00:21:56.420
What does it really mean?

00:21:56.420 --> 00:21:58.880
So I'm going to talk about
four different use cases.

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:01.190
And I've got demos for
two of these use cases.

00:22:01.190 --> 00:22:04.650
So just pay attention
to some of these demos.

00:22:04.650 --> 00:22:07.580
So the first one-- and the
first demo that I'll show you

00:22:07.580 --> 00:22:10.490
is the ability to have
consistent operations

00:22:10.490 --> 00:22:13.942
across the cloud and the prem.

00:22:13.942 --> 00:22:15.400
The demo that I'm
going to show you

00:22:15.400 --> 00:22:18.610
is how a single application
that's being developed,

00:22:18.610 --> 00:22:20.620
available in the
GCP marketplace,

00:22:20.620 --> 00:22:24.140
you can pick which cloud you
want to have that deployed to.

00:22:24.140 --> 00:22:25.840
And without changing
anything, you

00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:28.360
can just take that application
as it is and get it running.

00:22:28.360 --> 00:22:31.210
And you have a seamless
customer experience--

00:22:31.210 --> 00:22:34.190
a consistent experience
across the prem and the cloud.

00:22:34.190 --> 00:22:36.340
Now, this ability to have
the seamless experience

00:22:36.340 --> 00:22:39.130
also enables other
use cases for things

00:22:39.130 --> 00:22:42.050
like if you want to use
DevOps in the cloud,

00:22:42.050 --> 00:22:44.170
but you want to do
production in the premise.

00:22:44.170 --> 00:22:47.230
So you can do the development--

00:22:47.230 --> 00:22:51.040
so developers can build
in GKE, for example.

00:22:51.040 --> 00:22:53.273
And then IT may have
an IT policy that says,

00:22:53.273 --> 00:22:55.440
this application needs to
be deployed in the premise

00:22:55.440 --> 00:22:57.682
for sovereignty are
security reasons.

00:22:57.682 --> 00:22:59.140
And you can have
a simple IT policy

00:22:59.140 --> 00:23:00.328
that allows you to do it.

00:23:00.328 --> 00:23:01.870
But from a developer's
point of view,

00:23:01.870 --> 00:23:03.287
he doesn't have
to change anything

00:23:03.287 --> 00:23:06.110
when it's going into production,
because it is already IT ready.

00:23:06.110 --> 00:23:09.090
It's already data center ready.

00:23:09.090 --> 00:23:13.830
The third use case is one where
if there are services running

00:23:13.830 --> 00:23:16.740
in the premise that need
access to cloud services that

00:23:16.740 --> 00:23:19.860
are running in Google Cloud,
the Istio architecture actually

00:23:19.860 --> 00:23:24.300
allows seamless connectivity--
secure connectivity--

00:23:24.300 --> 00:23:27.010
between servers running in the
prem and Google Cloud Services.

00:23:27.010 --> 00:23:28.970
So here's an example
of either Cloud ML

00:23:28.970 --> 00:23:31.770
services or Big Data services
running in the cloud.

00:23:31.770 --> 00:23:34.057
And you've got seamless
access to that.

00:23:34.057 --> 00:23:34.890
So you unlock these.

00:23:34.890 --> 00:23:36.660
So you can still be running--
the application could still

00:23:36.660 --> 00:23:39.160
be running on the premise, but
you've got access to services

00:23:39.160 --> 00:23:40.670
that are running in the cloud.

00:23:40.670 --> 00:23:44.750
And the last one is where
we're talking about using

00:23:44.750 --> 00:23:46.130
the power of edge computing.

00:23:46.130 --> 00:23:47.150
And the second
demo that I'm going

00:23:47.150 --> 00:23:49.025
to show you will be
around the edge computing

00:23:49.025 --> 00:23:51.860
and how we can use
hybrid edge computing

00:23:51.860 --> 00:23:54.420
architectures, using machine
learning as an example.

00:23:54.420 --> 00:23:55.740
So I thought-- that's the
second demo that I'll do.

00:23:55.740 --> 00:23:58.010
So I'll show your use case
one and use case four--

00:23:58.010 --> 00:24:00.093
the two demos that I'm
going to be showing to you.

00:24:05.550 --> 00:24:09.810
So to just describe-- and this
is the Anthos architecture

00:24:09.810 --> 00:24:11.410
that I showed you
in the beginning.

00:24:11.410 --> 00:24:13.560
But again, I'm just using
this as a reference.

00:24:13.560 --> 00:24:18.210
So whether you use
Google CICP tools,

00:24:18.210 --> 00:24:21.600
using Spinnaker to be
able to build and develop

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:24.390
your own applications,
all of these

00:24:24.390 --> 00:24:28.110
are packaged applications,
available in the marketplace.

00:24:28.110 --> 00:24:30.840
What this allows you to do
is you can have the ability

00:24:30.840 --> 00:24:34.630
to deploy the application across
both the premise and the cloud.

00:24:34.630 --> 00:24:38.690
And that is what I'm
going to show you next.

00:24:38.690 --> 00:24:41.120
All right, so as
the demo kicks off--

00:24:41.120 --> 00:24:42.495
I had to put this
into the video,

00:24:42.495 --> 00:24:44.203
because if you show
the whole thing live,

00:24:44.203 --> 00:24:45.660
it does take a
little bit of time.

00:24:45.660 --> 00:24:47.493
So we put this into
video so we can actually

00:24:47.493 --> 00:24:50.130
run you through the demo.

00:24:50.130 --> 00:24:52.500
So to start the demo,
what we're going to start

00:24:52.500 --> 00:24:54.540
is first we are going
to create Kubernetes

00:24:54.540 --> 00:24:57.000
clusters on the
HyperFlex system,

00:24:57.000 --> 00:24:58.680
running inside the premise.

00:24:58.680 --> 00:25:02.400
And step two-- we're going to
register that cluster into GKE,

00:25:02.400 --> 00:25:04.760
so you can actually view
and manage that GKE cluster

00:25:04.760 --> 00:25:07.890
from GKE, even though
it's running on the prem.

00:25:07.890 --> 00:25:09.630
So the first step
in the installation

00:25:09.630 --> 00:25:11.340
is actually to get an
admin workstation running

00:25:11.340 --> 00:25:11.860
in the prem.

00:25:11.860 --> 00:25:14.460
And there's an install
process to which we get

00:25:14.460 --> 00:25:16.140
the admin workstation running.

00:25:16.140 --> 00:25:18.690
Once the admin workstation
is running inside the premise

00:25:18.690 --> 00:25:22.020
environment, you just create
a cluster create command

00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:23.550
from the admin console.

00:25:23.550 --> 00:25:25.680
And what this is
showing right now

00:25:25.680 --> 00:25:29.640
is that the cluster creation
completed in the background.

00:25:29.640 --> 00:25:31.410
So now what we've
got is we've got

00:25:31.410 --> 00:25:34.320
the cluster has been created,
running in the premise.

00:25:34.320 --> 00:25:35.970
Second, you actually go to GKE.

00:25:35.970 --> 00:25:38.460
So this is running
in the GKE console.

00:25:38.460 --> 00:25:40.710
And what we're doing
over here is we're now

00:25:40.710 --> 00:25:43.095
claiming that cluster into GKE.

00:25:43.095 --> 00:25:44.720
So there's a two-factor
authentication.

00:25:44.720 --> 00:25:48.400
You get the
authentication token,

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:51.202
you register that
cluster with the cloud.

00:25:51.202 --> 00:25:53.160
You're going to give it
some tags, because it's

00:25:53.160 --> 00:25:54.440
running in an RTP data center.

00:25:54.440 --> 00:25:55.898
And you've given
it a HyperFlex tag

00:25:55.898 --> 00:25:58.770
to signify that
this is HyperFlex.

00:25:58.770 --> 00:26:02.220
And if you actually see
here, in GKE, in the console,

00:26:02.220 --> 00:26:05.790
you're seeing a HyperFlex GKE
on-premise cluster, running

00:26:05.790 --> 00:26:08.010
in a data center environment.

00:26:08.010 --> 00:26:11.310
And you're seeing that side
by side with GKE clusters

00:26:11.310 --> 00:26:17.370
running in a US west
region of Google Cloud.

00:26:17.370 --> 00:26:19.440
So now in GKE,
what you've seen is

00:26:19.440 --> 00:26:23.460
that you'll see both data
center clusters and cloud based

00:26:23.460 --> 00:26:25.050
clusters are all running--

00:26:25.050 --> 00:26:31.430
are viewed in one single
dashboard in your GKE view.

00:26:31.430 --> 00:26:33.462
Now just to confirm
that this is indeed

00:26:33.462 --> 00:26:34.920
running on the
premise environment,

00:26:34.920 --> 00:26:36.545
you're going to do
a simple experiment.

00:26:36.545 --> 00:26:39.590
We look at the nodes that
are running in the premise.

00:26:39.590 --> 00:26:43.890
And we compare with the VMs that
are running in the HyperFlex

00:26:43.890 --> 00:26:44.688
environment.

00:26:44.688 --> 00:26:46.730
And you'll see that the
same worker of nodes that

00:26:46.730 --> 00:26:48.380
were created in the premise--

00:26:48.380 --> 00:26:50.180
there's a complete
match between that.

00:26:50.180 --> 00:26:52.970
Just if there's any
skeptics in the room--

00:26:52.970 --> 00:26:56.480
just to confirm that the
clusters and the VMs that

00:26:56.480 --> 00:26:59.540
have been created by Anthos
and you're seeing in GKE

00:26:59.540 --> 00:27:01.532
are the exact same
VMs and nodes.

00:27:01.532 --> 00:27:02.990
These are Kubernetes
nodes, running

00:27:02.990 --> 00:27:07.015
in a VM that is running on
the premise environment.

00:27:07.015 --> 00:27:08.640
Let's go to the last
stage of the demo.

00:27:08.640 --> 00:27:10.723
In the last stage of the
demo, what we're doing is

00:27:10.723 --> 00:27:13.030
we're going to take a
packaged application-- just

00:27:13.030 --> 00:27:13.780
to keep it simple.

00:27:13.780 --> 00:27:16.230
We're going to take a packaged
application from the GKE

00:27:16.230 --> 00:27:17.280
marketplace.

00:27:17.280 --> 00:27:19.990
To keep it simple, we've
used Nginx as the example.

00:27:19.990 --> 00:27:25.350
So we've taken an Nginx
from the GCP marketplace.

00:27:25.350 --> 00:27:27.810
And we're going to deploy
an instance of Nginx

00:27:27.810 --> 00:27:35.120
into the HyperFlex cluster
running in the premise.

00:27:35.120 --> 00:27:38.590
So what you'll see in
the next few steps is,

00:27:38.590 --> 00:27:42.370
when you go to the
marketplace and pick Nginx out

00:27:42.370 --> 00:27:45.490
of the marketplace, we
can pick the cluster

00:27:45.490 --> 00:27:46.510
to which you can deploy.

00:27:46.510 --> 00:27:49.930
So either you can pick a cluster
that's running in the cloud

00:27:49.930 --> 00:27:51.790
or pick the HyperFlex
cluster that

00:27:51.790 --> 00:27:55.930
is the GKE on-prem cluster,
running in the premise.

00:27:55.930 --> 00:27:58.870
Just change the namespace,
give it an instance ID,

00:27:58.870 --> 00:28:00.580
and click Deploy.

00:28:00.580 --> 00:28:02.720
And that's it.

00:28:02.720 --> 00:28:05.420
You have taken a
standard application,

00:28:05.420 --> 00:28:06.800
running in the
marketplace-- this

00:28:06.800 --> 00:28:09.300
could be a CICP-based
custom application as well.

00:28:09.300 --> 00:28:10.550
But it doesn't matter.

00:28:10.550 --> 00:28:12.050
Once you've built
the application

00:28:12.050 --> 00:28:14.600
and it's available in a
Kubernetes environment,

00:28:14.600 --> 00:28:16.970
getting this deployed and
running on the premise

00:28:16.970 --> 00:28:18.817
is a click of a button.

00:28:18.817 --> 00:28:20.650
So this is where the
game-changing attribute

00:28:20.650 --> 00:28:21.150
comes in.

00:28:21.150 --> 00:28:23.380
Because now you've truly
enabled consistency

00:28:23.380 --> 00:28:25.000
between the cloud and the prem.

00:28:25.000 --> 00:28:26.680
Because without changing a bit--

00:28:26.680 --> 00:28:28.120
without changing
a line of code--

00:28:28.120 --> 00:28:29.828
you have created a
consistent environment

00:28:29.828 --> 00:28:31.940
between the prem and the cloud.

00:28:31.940 --> 00:28:34.100
And just to confirm, right,
so what we're doing now

00:28:34.100 --> 00:28:36.830
is we've taken the external
IP of the Nginx system-- just

00:28:36.830 --> 00:28:37.850
logged into that.

00:28:37.850 --> 00:28:40.290
And you can see that
service is up and running.

00:28:40.290 --> 00:28:41.420
And this is running.

00:28:41.420 --> 00:28:43.330
It's an Nginx application.

00:28:43.330 --> 00:28:44.850
It's not a simple example.

00:28:44.850 --> 00:28:46.490
But basically what
we have shown is

00:28:46.490 --> 00:28:51.380
how we are able to take
applications developed in GKE

00:28:51.380 --> 00:28:55.130
and host that with
absolute simplicity

00:28:55.130 --> 00:28:57.710
into a cluster that's
running on the premise.

00:28:57.710 --> 00:28:58.910
So the demo was--

00:28:58.910 --> 00:29:01.520
the video was-- probably running
a little too fast for you

00:29:01.520 --> 00:29:02.460
to pay attention.

00:29:02.460 --> 00:29:05.480
So let me just slow down
and repeat this again.

00:29:05.480 --> 00:29:10.310
What you saw in the GKE
console was a set of clusters.

00:29:10.310 --> 00:29:14.240
Some of those clusters were
running in GKE in the cloud.

00:29:14.240 --> 00:29:16.880
Some of those clusters were
running in a data center.

00:29:16.880 --> 00:29:19.093
But it didn't matter
from GKE's point of view.

00:29:19.093 --> 00:29:21.260
It didn't matter from the
application point of view.

00:29:21.260 --> 00:29:23.135
Because now you can just
take the application

00:29:23.135 --> 00:29:25.253
and just pick which
instance-- or which cluster--

00:29:25.253 --> 00:29:26.420
where you want that running.

00:29:26.420 --> 00:29:28.490
And it creates a completely
seamless environment

00:29:28.490 --> 00:29:30.130
across both of them.

00:29:30.130 --> 00:29:33.520
Now Anthos is also going to be
running on other public clouds

00:29:33.520 --> 00:29:34.270
as well.

00:29:34.270 --> 00:29:35.920
So now you can take
that same example,

00:29:35.920 --> 00:29:37.990
and from the
marketplace, once you've

00:29:37.990 --> 00:29:40.120
claimed the cluster
running anywhere,

00:29:40.120 --> 00:29:43.330
you can deploy this on any
cloud, or on the premise,

00:29:43.330 --> 00:29:45.820
or on the edge, or
in Google Cloud.

00:29:45.820 --> 00:29:49.120
And it's completely seamless
from a developer and operations

00:29:49.120 --> 00:29:50.390
point of view.

00:29:50.390 --> 00:29:53.800
So this is really where we
think that this is opening up

00:29:53.800 --> 00:29:56.140
game-changing possibilities
for our customers that

00:29:56.140 --> 00:29:59.910
are looking to adopt
application modernization.

00:29:59.910 --> 00:30:02.070
Now let's go into
the second demo.

00:30:02.070 --> 00:30:05.940
All right, so in the case of
the second demo-- in this case,

00:30:05.940 --> 00:30:08.820
let me explain the demo
context a little bit before we

00:30:08.820 --> 00:30:11.310
go into details on this.

00:30:11.310 --> 00:30:14.070
So the background here is
this is a bolts application.

00:30:14.070 --> 00:30:17.448
Those of you who have
done any work on ML--

00:30:17.448 --> 00:30:18.990
this is a standard
application that's

00:30:18.990 --> 00:30:22.290
used to do a machine
learning demos.

00:30:22.290 --> 00:30:24.180
But the problem
statement is this.

00:30:24.180 --> 00:30:27.210
The problem statement is
this is a factory automation

00:30:27.210 --> 00:30:28.210
environment.

00:30:28.210 --> 00:30:30.840
It's an industrial plant.

00:30:30.840 --> 00:30:33.900
And the industrial plant is
using a variety of bolts.

00:30:33.900 --> 00:30:36.540
Now these bolts-- they
have different metrics.

00:30:36.540 --> 00:30:39.058
They have different
measurement dimensions.

00:30:39.058 --> 00:30:40.725
Right, they have the
standard dimension,

00:30:40.725 --> 00:30:42.630
there's a metric dimension.

00:30:42.630 --> 00:30:44.940
And to the visual
eye, you can hardly

00:30:44.940 --> 00:30:47.700
tell the difference
between these two bolts.

00:30:47.700 --> 00:30:53.730
But if you put the wrong
bolt into the machine,

00:30:53.730 --> 00:30:59.350
it could stop production--
it could ruin the equipment.

00:30:59.350 --> 00:31:02.415
So the ability to use the
right bolt in the right factory

00:31:02.415 --> 00:31:04.290
automation-- industrial
automation-- workflow

00:31:04.290 --> 00:31:05.430
is very important.

00:31:05.430 --> 00:31:08.070
Now if you leave this to humans
to be able to visually detect,

00:31:08.070 --> 00:31:08.580
it's really hard.

00:31:08.580 --> 00:31:10.080
Because as you can see,
the difference between them

00:31:10.080 --> 00:31:11.190
is very, very minuscule.

00:31:11.190 --> 00:31:13.570
It's hard to tell the difference
between the two of them.

00:31:13.570 --> 00:31:15.270
And so this is a
classic example where

00:31:15.270 --> 00:31:18.450
you can do real-time machine
learning at the factory floor

00:31:18.450 --> 00:31:20.690
in order to detect
the right bolt

00:31:20.690 --> 00:31:22.440
and make sure that the
right bolt is used.

00:31:22.440 --> 00:31:23.790
I'm just using the bolt
as an example, right.

00:31:23.790 --> 00:31:25.623
There are many, many
more use cases that get

00:31:25.623 --> 00:31:27.760
enabled to something like this.

00:31:27.760 --> 00:31:31.020
But the point is that we are
using the power of machine

00:31:31.020 --> 00:31:34.363
learning to be able to do
real-time image diagnosis

00:31:34.363 --> 00:31:36.780
and use the image diagnosis
to solve a particular business

00:31:36.780 --> 00:31:38.140
problem.

00:31:38.140 --> 00:31:40.890
And in this particular
case-- in this demo--

00:31:40.890 --> 00:31:42.660
what we are going
to show you is how

00:31:42.660 --> 00:31:47.020
we are building a hybrid
machine learning use case.

00:31:47.020 --> 00:31:49.320
So we have got data scientists
that have developed--

00:31:49.320 --> 00:31:52.360
I think the next slide goes
into that in more detail.

00:31:52.360 --> 00:31:55.825
So data scientists-- there's the
data sources that's available.

00:31:55.825 --> 00:31:57.700
There's typically the
stream of images that's

00:31:57.700 --> 00:31:59.212
available as a training data.

00:31:59.212 --> 00:32:00.920
You've got to collect
and clean the data.

00:32:00.920 --> 00:32:03.790
There's entire data pipeline
work that is required.

00:32:03.790 --> 00:32:06.170
The data scientist then
develops the model.

00:32:06.170 --> 00:32:09.383
There's training, and
then you develop a model.

00:32:09.383 --> 00:32:11.800
At the end, when you need to
run real-time in inferencing,

00:32:11.800 --> 00:32:15.195
that model does need to be
deployed into the premise--

00:32:15.195 --> 00:32:16.570
not the premise,
that model needs

00:32:16.570 --> 00:32:20.170
to be deployed wherever the
inferencing needs to happen.

00:32:20.170 --> 00:32:23.140
In this particular case,
we've used an example where

00:32:23.140 --> 00:32:25.210
the training and the
model development

00:32:25.210 --> 00:32:28.650
happens in Google
Cloud, using Kubeflow,

00:32:28.650 --> 00:32:31.780
and I'll describe Kubeflow
in a little bit more detail.

00:32:31.780 --> 00:32:33.280
But the inferencing
actually happens

00:32:33.280 --> 00:32:35.200
on a HyperFlex
edge system that's

00:32:35.200 --> 00:32:37.060
sitting in the factory floor.

00:32:37.060 --> 00:32:40.870
Now because of the real-time
nature of the inferencing,

00:32:40.870 --> 00:32:43.390
it's very difficult. Because
as the factory production

00:32:43.390 --> 00:32:46.030
line is moving--
as these equipment

00:32:46.030 --> 00:32:48.220
and the bolts are
coming to the line,

00:32:48.220 --> 00:32:51.693
the latency required
to process these means

00:32:51.693 --> 00:32:53.860
that it's difficult to send
some of this transaction

00:32:53.860 --> 00:32:55.527
over to the cloud or
to the data center.

00:32:55.527 --> 00:32:56.670
So this is the reason.

00:32:56.670 --> 00:32:58.540
So I talked about
some reasons why

00:32:58.540 --> 00:33:01.750
customers may choose to
have workloads running

00:33:01.750 --> 00:33:03.250
in the prem on the edge.

00:33:03.250 --> 00:33:04.355
I talked about security.

00:33:04.355 --> 00:33:05.480
I talked about sovereignty.

00:33:05.480 --> 00:33:07.300
I talked about data gravity.

00:33:07.300 --> 00:33:09.770
But latency is actually
a driving factor as well.

00:33:09.770 --> 00:33:11.260
So when you have
applications that

00:33:11.260 --> 00:33:14.090
require very low
latency access to data,

00:33:14.090 --> 00:33:17.860
sometimes it's not possible
to move the workload back

00:33:17.860 --> 00:33:18.630
to the cloud.

00:33:18.630 --> 00:33:22.630
So it's a classic use case-- why
a combination of Cloud and Edge

00:33:22.630 --> 00:33:26.027
is required to be able to
solve a customer problem.

00:33:26.027 --> 00:33:27.610
Now what's the
technology behind this?

00:33:27.610 --> 00:33:31.470
The technology behind this
is an open source project

00:33:31.470 --> 00:33:32.680
called Kubeflow.

00:33:32.680 --> 00:33:33.970
So Kubeflow is a project--

00:33:33.970 --> 00:33:35.970
it's another project where
Cisco and Google have

00:33:35.970 --> 00:33:37.267
collaborated very strongly.

00:33:37.267 --> 00:33:38.350
We have contributed a lot.

00:33:38.350 --> 00:33:41.720
We're the number two contributor
to Kubeflow after Google.

00:33:41.720 --> 00:33:42.940
But what is Kubeflow?

00:33:42.940 --> 00:33:44.560
Kubeflow is basically
an integration

00:33:44.560 --> 00:33:46.840
of TensorFlow and Kubernetes.

00:33:46.840 --> 00:33:49.540
It allows customers to
build TensorFlow models

00:33:49.540 --> 00:33:52.250
and deploy them into Kubernetes.

00:33:52.250 --> 00:33:55.010
In addition to the ability
to just run TensorFlow models

00:33:55.010 --> 00:33:56.870
and Kubernetes, it
also has the ability

00:33:56.870 --> 00:33:58.925
to manage data
pipelines as well.

00:33:58.925 --> 00:34:00.550
Now again, I don't
know how many of you

00:34:00.550 --> 00:34:03.980
are a data scientists or machine
learning engineers in the room,

00:34:03.980 --> 00:34:06.050
but one of the
biggest challenges

00:34:06.050 --> 00:34:08.510
you'll find that data scientists
are able to do the model

00:34:08.510 --> 00:34:09.050
development.

00:34:09.050 --> 00:34:10.820
It's relatively easy.

00:34:10.820 --> 00:34:11.989
It's still time consuming.

00:34:11.989 --> 00:34:13.679
You still need
time to develop it.

00:34:13.679 --> 00:34:16.580
But once you develop the
model, deploying that

00:34:16.580 --> 00:34:18.230
to where you need
the model to run

00:34:18.230 --> 00:34:19.820
is actually a very
complex problem.

00:34:19.820 --> 00:34:21.320
We've had customers
come and tell us

00:34:21.320 --> 00:34:23.070
that it can take six
months or even a year

00:34:23.070 --> 00:34:25.429
or sometimes to get the
models running efficiently

00:34:25.429 --> 00:34:27.895
in the infrastructure the way
they need the model to run.

00:34:27.895 --> 00:34:30.020
So this is the second
problem that Kubeflow solves.

00:34:30.020 --> 00:34:31.937
Because it gives you the
ability to have these

00:34:31.937 --> 00:34:33.409
reusable data pipelines.

00:34:33.409 --> 00:34:37.070
And very simple capabilities
to be able to run these data

00:34:37.070 --> 00:34:38.903
pipelines in a location
of your choice.

