00:39:06.620 --> 00:39:10.790
I'm going to move over next
to a customer case study.

00:39:10.790 --> 00:39:12.950
I'd like to talk about
a financial services

00:39:12.950 --> 00:39:15.070
customer called Keybank.

00:39:15.070 --> 00:39:18.110
So Keybank is a large financial
services organization.

00:39:18.110 --> 00:39:20.708
They're headquartered
in the Midwest.

00:39:20.708 --> 00:39:22.250
They're headquartered
in the Midwest,

00:39:22.250 --> 00:39:24.250
spreading their footprint
across 15 states--

00:39:24.250 --> 00:39:27.650
1,100 branches, close
to 20,000 employees,

00:39:27.650 --> 00:39:31.260
a fairly large financial
services corporation.

00:39:31.260 --> 00:39:35.760
They offer a whole host
of financial services.

00:39:35.760 --> 00:39:37.760
Now Keybank is one of the more--

00:39:37.760 --> 00:39:41.750
I would say-- one of the more
forward-looking enterprises

00:39:41.750 --> 00:39:42.633
in the world.

00:39:42.633 --> 00:39:44.050
They've had a
DevOps team and they

00:39:44.050 --> 00:39:45.883
started Kubernetes and
container development

00:39:45.883 --> 00:39:46.870
over four years ago.

00:39:46.870 --> 00:39:49.760
So they're one of the early
movers in this industry.

00:39:49.760 --> 00:39:51.560
So they are, I would
say, thought leaders

00:39:51.560 --> 00:39:53.390
when it comes to
Kubernetes adoption

00:39:53.390 --> 00:39:55.140
and converting traditional.

00:39:55.140 --> 00:39:57.890
You'd assume that the banks
and the financial services

00:39:57.890 --> 00:40:02.210
are more averse to moving
to some of the modernization

00:40:02.210 --> 00:40:03.060
technologies.

00:40:03.060 --> 00:40:04.200
They've been doing it
for over four years.

00:40:04.200 --> 00:40:07.040
So they've been using Kubernetes
for over four years right now.

00:40:07.040 --> 00:40:11.960
And what Keybank did is they
have picked Anthos on HyperFlex

00:40:11.960 --> 00:40:16.700
as their platform for deploying
their microservice applications

00:40:16.700 --> 00:40:18.450
in the premise data centers.

00:40:18.450 --> 00:40:21.200
Now because of
multiple reasons, they

00:40:21.200 --> 00:40:24.350
need to have an environment
that's available on premise.

00:40:24.350 --> 00:40:26.540
And first, they are big
Google Cloud customers.

00:40:26.540 --> 00:40:28.540
So they do a lot of
development on Google Cloud.

00:40:28.540 --> 00:40:30.060
They use GKE very heavily.

00:40:30.060 --> 00:40:33.165
And they first wanted
to build an environment

00:40:33.165 --> 00:40:34.790
where they can build
these applications

00:40:34.790 --> 00:40:38.030
and run these applications
in the premise.

00:40:38.030 --> 00:40:40.712
And one of the things that
Anthos gives this customers

00:40:40.712 --> 00:40:42.170
is the ability to
monitor and check

00:40:42.170 --> 00:40:44.840
the health of the application,
using the tools that we talked

00:40:44.840 --> 00:40:48.740
about like StackDriver,
which allows you to monitor

00:40:48.740 --> 00:40:50.310
the health of the application.

00:40:50.310 --> 00:40:53.210
But the use case--
really, for them,

00:40:53.210 --> 00:40:56.120
the reason why a
hybrid environment

00:40:56.120 --> 00:40:57.950
is very important to
them is because they

00:40:57.950 --> 00:40:59.910
want to be able to
burst to the cloud.

00:40:59.910 --> 00:41:01.800
So when they build
their infrastructure,

00:41:01.800 --> 00:41:04.910
they size it for average load.

00:41:04.910 --> 00:41:08.090
But during peak
loads, they don't

00:41:08.090 --> 00:41:10.940
want to build large data
centers to cater for peak load.

00:41:10.940 --> 00:41:13.070
So they want to be able
to burst into the cloud

00:41:13.070 --> 00:41:14.040
when they need to.

00:41:14.040 --> 00:41:18.330
But the average load is running
in the data center environment.

00:41:18.330 --> 00:41:21.440
So this kind of
seamless infrastructure,

00:41:21.440 --> 00:41:23.952
where you have both the premise
environment and the cloud,

00:41:23.952 --> 00:41:26.410
and you can burst into the
cloud without changing anything,

00:41:26.410 --> 00:41:26.710
right.

00:41:26.710 --> 00:41:28.570
So you can have
auto-scaling capabilities.

00:41:28.570 --> 00:41:30.153
And with the auto
scaling capabilities

00:41:30.153 --> 00:41:32.090
built into the app,
you can build the logic

00:41:32.090 --> 00:41:35.450
in to say, just auto-scale this
into GCP during times of load,

00:41:35.450 --> 00:41:37.910
which allows them to have
the seamless environment.

00:41:37.910 --> 00:41:40.310
So it's another
use case of why it

00:41:40.310 --> 00:41:43.550
is important to have a
uniform, consistent environment

00:41:43.550 --> 00:41:45.385
across the board.

00:41:45.385 --> 00:41:47.010
Now why did they pick
HyperFlex, right?

00:41:47.010 --> 00:41:48.930
So the reason they
picked HyperFlex

00:41:48.930 --> 00:41:52.050
was because they wanted a
simple, private cloud to be

00:41:52.050 --> 00:41:54.345
able to run GKE on premise.

00:41:54.345 --> 00:41:55.900
So they wanted it to be simple.

00:41:55.900 --> 00:41:58.650
They wanted to have all of the
infrastructure capabilities

00:41:58.650 --> 00:42:00.417
built into this platform--

00:42:00.417 --> 00:42:01.500
very simple to install it.

00:42:01.500 --> 00:42:04.200
In fact, its entire
POC was completed

00:42:04.200 --> 00:42:07.190
in less than two weeks time.

00:42:07.190 --> 00:42:10.830
We sent them a HyperFlex
cluster as an initial POC.

00:42:10.830 --> 00:42:16.580
They brought up the HyperFlex
system in a few hours.

00:42:16.580 --> 00:42:20.730
They deployed GKE on-prem
onto that cluster.

00:42:20.730 --> 00:42:23.080
And they started migrating
their Kubernetes application

00:42:23.080 --> 00:42:24.460
to this environment.

00:42:24.460 --> 00:42:27.270
This POC has completed
successfully.

00:42:27.270 --> 00:42:28.950
The speed at which
the POC completed

00:42:28.950 --> 00:42:30.935
and the ease at which
they were able to do it--

00:42:30.935 --> 00:42:32.310
it goes back to
some other things

00:42:32.310 --> 00:42:33.768
that we said in
the past, which was

00:42:33.768 --> 00:42:35.640
our goal is how
easy do we make it

00:42:35.640 --> 00:42:37.860
for customers to be able
to build these cloud

00:42:37.860 --> 00:42:39.260
environments on premise.

00:42:39.260 --> 00:42:41.430
So it's a really
simple environment

00:42:41.430 --> 00:42:43.333
to be able to bring
up these applications.

00:42:43.333 --> 00:42:45.000
And of course, for
them, the reliability

00:42:45.000 --> 00:42:46.500
was highly important
as well, right.

00:42:46.500 --> 00:42:49.500
Because of the clustered nature
of the HyperFlex architecture,

00:42:49.500 --> 00:42:50.730
where there is zero downtime.

00:42:50.730 --> 00:42:53.160
Application upgrades can
happen without requiring

00:42:53.160 --> 00:42:53.873
cluster downtime.

00:42:53.873 --> 00:42:56.040
Infrastructure upgrades can
happen without requiring

00:42:56.040 --> 00:42:57.120
cluster downtime.

00:42:57.120 --> 00:43:02.580
You can do firmware upgrades.

00:43:02.580 --> 00:43:04.590
You can do storage
upgrades-- all of that

00:43:04.590 --> 00:43:06.370
while applications
continue to run.

00:43:06.370 --> 00:43:08.390
So the ability to do
zero downtime upgrades

00:43:08.390 --> 00:43:09.390
was extremely important.

00:43:09.390 --> 00:43:11.817
So really, this is, again,
bringing one of those cloud

00:43:11.817 --> 00:43:12.900
attributes to the premise.

00:43:12.900 --> 00:43:15.430
Because now, this is a cloud,
and that doesn't go down.

00:43:15.430 --> 00:43:16.830
Even if you're doing things
in the infrastructure,

00:43:16.830 --> 00:43:18.090
the cluster always stays up.

00:43:18.090 --> 00:43:19.410
Applications always stay up.

00:43:19.410 --> 00:43:21.880
So it is a zero
downtime environment

00:43:21.880 --> 00:43:25.470
for customers looking to get
that cloud experience on prem.

00:43:25.470 --> 00:43:29.340
So this is a great
example of a customer that

00:43:29.340 --> 00:43:32.280
